1
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:04,290
Narrator: The Law Behind the Deal.

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:07,415
Narrator: Episode 5. The Contract Across the Ocean

3
00:00:11,115 --> 00:00:17,268
Kito: Hans emailed a price for five packing machines. I replied with a few changes. Do we have a contract yet?

4
00:00:17,418 --> 00:00:21,518
Nia: Hamburg to Dar es Salaam. That's an international sale of goods.

5
00:00:21,668 --> 00:00:35,266
Professor Lex: Which brings us to the CISG, the UN Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods, agreed in Vienna in 1980. More than ninety countries have joined it.

6
00:00:35,316 --> 00:00:42,706
Professor Lex: It gives traders one set of rules for forming the contract, and for the rights and duties of seller and buyer.

7
00:00:46,006 --> 00:00:54,399
Professor Lex: The Convention applies to contracts for the sale of goods between parties whose places of business are in different states.

8
00:00:54,449 --> 00:00:57,489
Professor Lex: Route A: both states are Contracting States.

9
00:00:57,489 --> 00:01:03,872
Professor Lex: Route B: the rules of private international law lead to the law of a Contracting State.

10
00:01:04,122 --> 00:01:10,641
Professor Lex: Nationality is irrelevant. What matters is where each party has its place of business.

11
00:01:10,791 --> 00:01:14,557
Nia: But Tanzania hasn't joined the Convention, and Germany has.

12
00:01:14,607 --> 00:01:22,501
Professor Lex: So route A fails. But if the contract chooses German law, route B applies, and German law includes the CISG.

13
00:01:22,651 --> 00:01:29,361
Professor Lex: And remember, the parties can also exclude the Convention, or vary its rules, in their contract.

14
00:01:32,861 --> 00:01:35,820
Professor Lex: Some sales are left out completely.

15
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:43,634
Professor Lex: Consumer sales, for personal, family or household use, unless the seller neither knew nor ought to have known that.

16
00:01:43,634 --> 00:01:48,310
Professor Lex: Sales by auction, or on execution by legal authority.

17
00:01:48,310 --> 00:01:52,381
Professor Lex: Shares, securities, negotiable instruments, and money.

18
00:01:52,381 --> 00:01:54,939
Professor Lex: Ships, hovercraft and aircraft.

19
00:01:54,939 --> 00:01:56,647
Professor Lex: And electricity.

20
00:01:56,897 --> 00:02:09,190
Professor Lex: Goods to be manufactured do count as sales, unless the buyer supplies a substantial part of the materials. And contracts that are mainly for labour or services are excluded.

21
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:20,594
Professor Lex: Finally, the Convention does not deal with whether the contract is valid, who owns the goods, or liability for death or personal injury. National law covers those.

22
00:02:24,294 --> 00:02:28,398
Professor Lex: A few general rules run through the whole Convention.

23
00:02:28,448 --> 00:02:36,666
Professor Lex: Interpret it with regard to its international character, the need for uniformity, and good faith in international trade.

24
00:02:36,666 --> 00:02:42,785
Professor Lex: Gaps are filled by its general principles, and only then by the applicable national law.

25
00:02:42,785 --> 00:02:51,347
Professor Lex: The parties' statements are read by their intent, if the other side knew it, or else as a reasonable person would understand them.

26
00:02:51,347 --> 00:02:58,613
Professor Lex: Parties are bound by usages they agreed to, and by practices they have established between themselves.

27
00:02:58,613 --> 00:03:05,583
Professor Lex: And a sales contract need not be in writing. It can be proved by any means, even by witnesses.

28
00:03:05,733 --> 00:03:08,779
Kito: So even a phone call with Hans could create a contract!

29
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,646
Professor Lex: A contract needs an offer, and an acceptance.

30
00:03:15,696 --> 00:03:29,061
Professor Lex: An offer is a proposal addressed to one or more specific people. It must be sufficiently definite, naming the goods and fixing the quantity and the price, and it must show an intention to be bound.

31
00:03:29,211 --> 00:03:36,481
Professor Lex: Adverts, catalogues and price lists sent to the public are usually just invitations to make offers.

32
00:03:36,631 --> 00:03:40,116
Professor Lex: An offer takes effect when it reaches the offeree.

33
00:03:40,116 --> 00:03:44,982
Professor Lex: It can be withdrawn if the withdrawal arrives first, or at the same time.

34
00:03:44,982 --> 00:03:56,930
Professor Lex: It can be revoked until the offeree sends an acceptance. But not if the offer states a fixed time for acceptance, says it is irrevocable, or the offeree reasonably relied on it.

35
00:03:56,930 --> 00:04:00,857
Professor Lex: And a rejection ends the offer when it reaches the offeror.

36
00:04:01,007 --> 00:04:10,105
Hans: My email offered five machines at forty thousand euros each, open for fourteen days. So I can't revoke it during that time.

37
00:04:13,605 --> 00:04:21,447
Professor Lex: Acceptance is a statement, or conduct, showing assent. Silence or inactivity alone is never acceptance.

38
00:04:21,497 --> 00:04:33,326
Professor Lex: It takes effect when it reaches the offeror, within the time fixed, or a reasonable time. An oral offer must be accepted immediately, unless the circumstances show otherwise.

39
00:04:33,376 --> 00:04:38,339
Professor Lex: The contract is concluded at the moment the acceptance takes effect.

40
00:04:38,589 --> 00:04:45,705
Professor Lex: Now the trap. A reply that adds to, or changes, the offer is a rejection plus a counter-offer.

41
00:04:45,755 --> 00:04:53,778
Professor Lex: Unless the changes are immaterial. Then it is still an acceptance, unless the offeror objects without undue delay.

42
00:04:53,828 --> 00:05:03,282
Professor Lex: Changes to price, payment, quality, quantity, place and time of delivery, liability, or dispute settlement are always material.

43
00:05:03,432 --> 00:05:06,586
Kito: I replied: we accept, but at thirty-eight thousand euros.

44
00:05:06,636 --> 00:05:12,287
Nia: That changes the price. So it's a counter-offer, and there's no contract until Hans accepts it.

45
00:05:16,187 --> 00:05:22,899
The Auditor: Quick check. Kito replies, we accept, but the price must be thirty-eight thousand euros. What is that reply?

46
00:05:27,949 --> 00:05:30,171
The Auditor: A rejection, and a counter-offer.

47
00:05:30,321 --> 00:05:34,587
Professor Lex: A change to the price is always a material alteration.

48
00:05:38,537 --> 00:05:43,491
The Auditor: And this one. Can the CISG govern a sale between a Tanzanian buyer and a German seller?

49
00:05:48,541 --> 00:05:51,582
The Auditor: Yes, if the conflict rules lead to German law.

50
00:05:51,732 --> 00:05:59,850
Professor Lex: That's Article 1, paragraph 1 b. The law of a Contracting State applies, and that law includes the Convention.

51
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:05,504
Nia: Different states, places of business.

52
00:06:05,554 --> 00:06:09,198
Nia: Two routes: both Contracting States, or conflict rules.

53
00:06:09,248 --> 00:06:13,300
Nia: Exclusions: consumers, auctions, shares, ships, electricity.

54
00:06:13,350 --> 00:06:16,500
Nia: Offer must be definite. Revocation has limits.

55
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:18,855
Nia: Material change means counter-offer.

56
00:06:18,905 --> 00:06:28,442
Professor Lex: Next time, Hans and Kito must choose how the machines travel, who pays for the voyage, and where the risk passes. That means Incoterms.

57
00:06:28,492 --> 00:06:31,388
Captain Cargo: Eleven little codes, and every one of them matters!

58
00:06:31,738 --> 00:06:35,899
Narrator: Next time: Episode 6. Incoterms: Who Pays, Who Risks.

